[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-41", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 41 — keyed scans as vectors, plans as prepared statements; 64 slots 4797 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-41.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 41 - keyed scans as vectors, plans as prepared statements\n\n> Seed: iteration 40's S32 (the generic join's binding-set machinery).\n> Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung :sf1-full; SUT levi (M4 4P+6E, 16\n> threads), driver judah, LAN. Per-op costs measured at saturation only\n> (iteration 40's rule).\n\n## Generate / Rank\n\n  Per-op cost at saturation before: IS3 6.3 ms, IC09 5.3, IC07 4.8, IC08\n  0.68, IC02 0.57. IS3 -- a person's friends with names, no limit, 882\n  rows for the first parameter -- is now the heaviest op, and its\n  hand-written twin answers in 0.72: the generic join is 8x the index\n  path on a five-clause keyed join.\n\n  JFR with 40-deep stacks on IS3 alone: join-clause 63% of the engine's\n  samples, of which the keyed scan (`scan*` -> `datalog.query/query`)\n  41% -- a SET of {:s :p :o} maps built per scan, hasheq of every map,\n  hash-map assoc, the `for` lazy seq -- and query planning 12%\n  (`plan-clause-order` over five clauses on every request).\n\n  Two changes, each a few lines:\n    - datalog 8dc45c75: `query-vec`, an index scan as an eager vector\n      (an index yields distinct quads; the set removed nothing). `query`\n      keeps its set contract; the engine's keyed path takes the vector.\n    - kotobase-peer: the plan is cached by query VALUE, like a prepared\n      statement, refreshed every 1,024 uses. A plan is an order, never a\n      filter: a cached plan is never wrong, only possibly stale.\n\n  After: IS3 5.8, IC09 5.1, IC07 4.0, IC08 0.40, IC02 0.58, IS2 0.12.\n\n    rung sf1-full  slots  threads  reads/s  upd/s  on-time%  thru/s\n      it.39          64     16      2944     1475   100.00    4374.1\n      it.41          64     16      3328     1475   100.00    4797.0   (3712: 63.0% fails)\n\n  Gate 1,082/0; read-after-write 360/0/0. Tests: datalog JVM 137/464/0,\n  cljs 141/475/0; kotobase-peer 263/808/0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - The two families the iteration-39 profile named are being paid\n    down one term at a time; the term left is the binding\n    representation itself (maps hashed into sets at every clause) --\n    the 8x to the twin on IS3 is where it shows (S33).\n  - The per-op numbers moved less than the sweep did (+9.7%): the\n    scan set and the planner were paid by EVERY op, including the\n    seven cheap ones that make up most of the mix.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH (64-slot column 4374 ->\n4797 on the same rung, mix, host and threads). Ratio to the audited\nNo.1: 29.\n\nSeeds for iteration 42, in order:\n\n  - S33: the binding representation in join-clause (the 8x to the twin).\n  - M4: performance cores. S26: full-data parameters. S23b: IC05. M3: SF3.\n"}]
